{
"dur": 58.392,
"shots": [
{
"i": 1,
"start": 0.0,
"vo_start": 0.6,
"vo_end": 10.16,
"text": "And Tesla isn't the only one turning cars into phones. Mercedes went even further. Its MBUX Hyperscreen is glass from pillar to pillar, with a screen just for the passenger.",
"end": 10.435
},
{
"i": 2,
"start": 10.435,
"vo_start": 10.71,
"vo_end": 23.592,
"text": "Some models even get a dashboard camera for video calls. Yes, you can join the Monday meeting from the driver's seat. And another camera watches your eyes, to check you're not distracted. In a cabin that glows like a nightclub.",
"end": 23.867
},
{
"i": 3,
"start": 23.867,
"vo_start": 24.142,
"vo_end": 36.422,
"text": "So why push screens so hard? The honest answer is money. A good switch costs real engineering: moving parts, wiring, that lovely solid click. A touchscreen is one part that does everything.",
"end": 36.697
},
{
"i": 4,
"start": 36.697,
"vo_start": 36.972,
"vo_end": 44.932,
"text": "And once your car has a screen, it can show you anything. Ford has filed a patent for adverts inside the car, based on where you are and where you're going.",
"end": 45.207
},
{
"i": 5,
"start": 45.207,
"vo_start": 45.482,
"vo_end": 57.442,
"text": "Ford says a patent isn't a plan. But some Jeep owners have already reported full-screen pop-up ads at traffic lights. Don't like the ads? I'm sure there'll be an ad-free subscription.",
"end": 58.392
}
],
